{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"d4f0f11f0dd5d3e1993c87c970ea12e9a907b5291a574b62e6dec382c2f1a2c5","slug":"index","location":"/intro.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Source Code: Reverse Engineering the Atom","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Jin Lei","given":"Jin","family":"Lei"},"name":"Jin Lei","url":"https://jinlei.fewbody.com","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"github":"https://github.com/jinleiphys/atomic","source_url":"https://github.com/jinleiphys/atomic/blob/main/book/en/intro.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/jinleiphys/atomic/edit/main/book/en/intro.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"intro.md","url":"/atomic/en/build/intro-c4a6455c6e5381ae26b4ac31cbea2e95.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"thematicBreak","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"vxp5Jdrzp9"},{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The universe is a vast “black box,” and the atom is its fundamental logical unit.\nThis book attempts to reverse-engineer that black box from two perspectives: carbon-based intuition and silicon-based computation.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"v4rSsql4iN"}],"key":"AkDQrzT5Yl"}],"key":"C7yiSKee6f"},{"type":"thematicBreak","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"Bq8XOQzcAF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Imagine a black box sitting in front of you. You have no idea what is inside, and you cannot open it. The only thing you can do is feed something into it, a beam of light, a particle, a pulse of energy, and then observe what comes out: a spectral line, a scattering angle, a probability distribution. You collect thousands upon thousands of such input-output pairs, and then you ask yourself: what mechanism is hidden inside the box?","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"YLilwi3jKP"}],"key":"XDbGpgq4wg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is precisely what physicists have been doing for the past two centuries. Atoms are too small to observe directly; you cannot slice one open and inspect its interior, so you must probe it through experiments. Rutherford bombarded gold foil with alpha particles and recorded the scattering angle of every single particle. Bohr stared at the precise data of the hydrogen emission spectrum, searching for patterns. Heisenberg and Schrodinger, confronted with ever more refined experimental results, erected the mathematical framework of quantum mechanics. Every time, the process was the same: design an experiment, collect data, propose a model, use the model to predict new experimental outcomes, and if the prediction fails, revise the model and repeat the cycle.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"lBf8GKcJYY"}],"key":"AqAOqL0gOY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This process goes by another name you are surely familiar with: ","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"tOANRQBO6v"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"machine learning","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"esK0OHiZjA"}],"key":"DA6hA4RMkw"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"DLyzujj4rC"}],"key":"bZZeJoFz8T"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Training a neural network today is, at its core, the very same activity. You prepare a large set of input-output data pairs, design a model architecture, and let the algorithm adjust the model’s parameters so that its output matches the real data as closely as possible. After training, you use the model to predict new data it has never seen; if the predictions are off, you adjust the architecture or the data and retrain. Physicists call this “discovering the laws of nature,” and machine learning engineers call it “fitting data,” but strip away the terminological shell and they are the same cognitive activity: ","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"NNH0W3Vktg"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"constructing a model that generalizes from finite observations","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"SJjtf9qnQJ"}],"key":"pPnsEwEfQu"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"XVdD3dvYHy"}],"key":"N6st2WXMkh"},{"type":"mermaid","value":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Experimental Data / Training Data\"] --> B[\"Propose Model / Design Architecture\"]\n    B --> C[\"Validate Predictions / Test Generalization\"]\n    C -->|\"If failed, revise model\"| B\n    C --> D[\"Physical Laws / Trained Model\"]\n\n    style A fill:none,stroke:#333\n    style B fill:none,stroke:#333\n    style C fill:none,stroke:#333\n    style D fill:none,stroke:#333","key":"j26U2oUWtg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Differences certainly exist. The models physicists seek must be sufficiently concise, expressible in a handful of equations, capable of revealing the underlying causal mechanism. Neural networks, by contrast, are often enormous black boxes with billions of parameters: you know they work, but you can hardly articulate why. Yet this difference is one of degree, not of kind. Newton’s law of universal gravitation is an extraordinarily concise model: a single formula, ","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"x2jfk9iisB"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"F = Gm_1m_2/r^2","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F = Gm_1m_2/r^2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">F</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">r</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"ZssyCVTPIo"},{"type":"text","value":", fits all the data from a falling apple to the orbits of planets. The Schrodinger equation is a more complex model, but it too was “learned” from experimental data, only the learner was the human brain rather than gradient descent on a GPU.","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"McNVa57jcr"}],"key":"YuEP7ok75W"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Atomic physics is the most dramatic chapter in this cognitive adventure. It is here that humanity first discovered that the language our intuition uses to understand the world, continuous trajectories, definite positions, causally transparent evolution, breaks down entirely at the microscopic scale. The classical “model architecture” could not fit the data no matter how its parameters were tuned, and we had no choice but to invent an entirely new architecture: quantum mechanics. This was not an incremental improvement but a paradigm reconstruction, like jumping from decision trees to deep neural networks, where the underlying assumptions are completely different.","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"vegUGJZHp6"}],"key":"IYDJF8H1pY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The title of this book, “Source Code,” is a metaphor for this very cognitive process. The universe runs some invisible “program,” and physicists collect its inputs and outputs through experiments, then attempt to reverse-engineer the program’s logic. To study atomic physics is not to memorize a set of prepackaged conclusions, but to trace how those conclusions were “trained” out of data. They were trained by human intuition, mathematics, and countless rounds of trial and error.","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"pnJIPS9Bsq"}],"key":"iaEC0p3Vfn"},{"type":"thematicBreak","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"MtE4yqZxVn"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dual Narrative: Intuition and Algorithm","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"UMYtHccxL4"}],"identifier":"dual-narrative-intuition-and-algorithm","label":"Dual Narrative: Intuition and Algorithm","html_id":"dual-narrative-intuition-and-algorithm","implicit":true,"key":"rekinmuq7Z"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This book takes the rigorous framework of Yang Fujia’s ","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"aQCEFNQkSq"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Atomic Physics","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"rv7BfRpzvg"}],"key":"X1tkRDBBIl"},{"type":"text","value":" as its backbone, but adopts a different mode of reconstruction: a dual narrative.","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"xkdVqyA5EK"}],"key":"o8fYMUONEP"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Carbon Story (Carbon-based Intuition)","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"F2Uatm0OJk"}],"identifier":"id-1-carbon-story-carbon-based-intuition","label":"1. Carbon Story (Carbon-based Intuition)","html_id":"id-1-carbon-story-carbon-based-intuition","implicit":true,"key":"CutqeQ84Vd"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is where the warmth of human intelligence resides. Every breakthrough in physics was not a bolt of inspiration from the heavens, but a choice made by an individual or a group caught between evidence and bewilderment. Rutherford’s astonishment when he saw alpha particles scattered at large angles. Bohr’s all-or-nothing gamble in proposing the quantization postulate atop the ruins of classical physics. This narrative thread traces how concepts evolved and how paradigms were replaced. It is the rigorous knowledge thread essential for mastering the subject, and the core arc for understanding the ideas of physics.","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"GVigTnaMmu"}],"key":"j67V5yLEUS"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Silicon Perspective (Silicon-based Perspective)","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"bovnTQNDgb"}],"identifier":"id-2-silicon-perspective-silicon-based-perspective","label":"2. Silicon Perspective (Silicon-based Perspective)","html_id":"id-2-silicon-perspective-silicon-based-perspective","implicit":true,"key":"osbVNR8Spv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If we regard the laws of physics as the universe’s compression algorithms, many seemingly mysterious concepts take on a different appearance:","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"YptLqWnpYa"}],"key":"DCCGyZPmQV"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quantization","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"ATunj0XuD9"}],"key":"XGRg2qo4wi"},{"type":"text","value":": no longer mysticism, but ","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"lQ9vCmZzJd"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"sampling and quantization","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"bZsx8gJHEi"}],"key":"oDtPPgtxto"},{"type":"text","value":" from signal processing.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"NbJiUiQFrn"}],"key":"YINcLV3VYf"}],"key":"DQFXGgz44D"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"str